{"availability":"available","full":"zjoooooo/galaxy-explorer","name":"galaxy-explorer","description":"🌌 An interactive, procedurally-generated Milky Way galaxy in the browser — WebGL / three.js, no backend, no build step. MIT licensed.","category":"Design","language":"HTML","stars":96,"forks":8,"created":"2026-07-04T03:03:32Z","updated":"2026-08-04T16:32:18Z","license":null,"branch":"main","homepage":"https://zjoooooo.github.io/galaxy-explorer/","topics":["astronomy","creative-coding","galaxy","generative-art","procedural-generation","shaders","space","threejs","visualization","webgl"],"demo":"https://zjoooooo.github.io/galaxy-explorer/","color":"#63362a","ink":"#f8c7a8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T18:00:04.342Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-09T18:00:04.342Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-09T18:00:04.342Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-09T18:00:04.342Z","stars":96,"forks":8}],"technologies":["Three.js"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["An interactive, procedurally-generated Milky Way galaxy — with a full 3D Solar System, roaming comets, and real radio-galaxy jets — that runs entirely in the browser with WebGL (three.js). No backend, no build step, no dependencies to install, no API keys — just open the page and explore.","The galaxy itself is generated in code at load time: no image assets — the spiral arms, nebula gas, star clusters, distant galaxies and supernovae are all math. The Solar System layers real NASA-based surface textures on top for the planets, moons and Sun.","Free for study, teaching and research — personal projects, classrooms, universities and museums — under the PolyForm Noncommercial License 1.0.0. Commercial use requires a separate licence; please open an issue to arrange one."],"features":["Spiral disc — 85,000 dust points on four density-wave arms, with a warm","core fading to cool blue arms, bright star knots, HII regions and blue clusters.","Volumetric-looking nebula — a continuous fBm + Worley noise shader field","(domain-warped